{"ratings":[{"id":"C1","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A successful targeted gate can falsely satisfy an acceptance criterion when a requested test is missing, runs no tests, or matches another file."},{"id":"C2","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A quoted test path can be dropped during parsing, allowing an unrelated passing suite to satisfy the criterion."},{"id":"C3","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A gate setup can modify source bytes so tests pass, then cleanup restores failing bytes while the factory attributes the result to the commit."},{"id":"C4","consequence":"resume","trigger":"unusual","reason":"A crash in the narrow gap between saving gate results and marking the stage successful leaves cached evidence permanently unusable on resume."},{"id":"C2b","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"Passing the extracted test command can satisfy an entire criterion even when a separate required verification step remains unchecked."}]}